import marks "sngl:internal/marks"

// A top-level window — the entry point of a UI. `href` is the route the
// window is served at; `title` names it; `favicon` is its icon. `@error` is
// the window's outermost error boundary.
//
// `params` is what the route hands the body per request, as one struct value,
// and `T` is inferred from it: a path with `{name}` placeholders in it is
// served with a field of that name filled in from the URL. A window that takes
// none writes nothing, and `T` falls back to the struct with no fields.
//
// The body that reads them is the population of `content`, written by name
// the way any scoped slot's is — `component content(v) { … }` — and `v` is
// the caller's word. A body written as bare children sees no parameters at
// all: there is nowhere in a spread to write a name for them.
//
// That is why the path is a plain string here where it used to be an
// interpolation: the names in it are the struct's fields rather than
// identifiers in scope, so a path parameter and a node `#id` no longer share
// one namespace with nothing declaring either. Write the path with a
// backticked string, or escape the braces.
//
// Reading `name.href` / `name.title` off a `window #name` declaration
// lets links survive a window's path change without copying the path
// string. The #[builtin("window")] mark is how the checker recognises a
// window declaration — by tag, not by matching the name "window".
//
// A program must declare at least one: the package body renders nothing on
// its own, so a program with no window has nowhere to draw.
//
// `@close` runs when the user closes the window through the window manager,
// on a target whose windows the user can close (gtk4 and fyne). The handler
// says what a close means and the tree decides whether the window still
// exists: one under `if details` whose `@close` writes `details = false` is
// destroyed, and one whose handler writes nothing stays on screen. A window
// with no `@close` is hidden -- `open` brings it back -- and a close that
// leaves no window on screen ends the program, unless it started with none.
#[marks.builtin("window")]
component window<T = struct {}>(
    title string,
    href string,
    favicon string,
    params T,
    @error error,
    @close(),
    content ...component(v T) node,
) root {}

// Puts the window on screen and in front, on a target that holds windows the
// program can show and hide: gtk4 and fyne. Reached through the window's
// `#id` -- `window #details(…)`, then `details.open()` from a handler -- which
// is what lets a program started with no window on screen, one whose `@run`
// never called `run`, bring one up in answer to something.
#[marks.intrinsic("window.open")]
func window.open()

// Takes the window off screen without ending the program; `open` brings it
// back as it was.
#[marks.intrinsic("window.close")]
func window.close()
